  "S6": {
   "id": "S6",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Lecture Notes",
   "text": "4. Sampling and Confidence Intervals A population is the entire group of interest and a sample is a subset used to learn about it. Simple random sampling gives every member an equal chance of selection; stratified sampling samples within subgroups; convenience sampling is easy but prone to bias. Sampling bias occurs when the sample systematically differs from the population. The sampling distribution of the mean describes how sample means vary from sample to sample. The Central Limit Theorem states that for sufficiently large n the distribution of the sample mean is approximately normal with mean mu and standard error sigma / sqrt(n), regardless of the shape of the original population. The standard error shrinks as the sample size grows. A confidence interval gives a range of plausible values for a parameter. A 95% confidence interval for",
